{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HrbCmPPCTBgveJWAxkQYW5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — persuasion_turn_jailbreak_detect\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (ARR submission)\n5\t- Deadline: 2026-05-25 (ARR May cycle)\n6\t- Track: LLM Security / Discourse and Pragmatics\n7\t- Commitment deadline: 2026-08-02\n8\t\n9\t## Title (Working)\n10\tDisPeD: Persuasion-Aware Fine-Tuning for Robust Cross-Attack Multi-Turn Jailbreak Detection\n11\t\n12\t## Core Insight\n13\tPrior work established that persuasion is systematic in jailbreaks (Zeng et al. 2024), universal in successful attacks (Hagendorff 2026), and extractable from representations (arXiv 2508.05625). DisPeD operationalizes these observations: fine-tuning DeBERTa on an 8-class persuasion taxonomy as a multi-task objective produces turn embeddings that capture attack-relevant pragmatic features. The key finding is that vanilla semantic temporal modeling (the DeepContext-like approach: generic encoder + GRU) catastrophically fails on unseen attack types (F1=0.28 on DD), while persuasion-aware fine-tuning enables robust cross-attack generalization (F1=1.0). Even scrambled strategy labels achieve F1=0.98, confirming that the fine-tuning process on persuasion-related text, not specific label assignments, is the critical ingredient. The 8-class taxonomy additionally provides native interpretability via strategy trajectory analysis.\n14\t\n15\t## Novelty Positioning (Dream-informed)\n16\tNOT: \"We discover that persuasion patterns exist in jailbreaks\" (already known).\n17\tNOT: \"An evasion hierarchy exists: lexical < semantic < pragmatic\" (TF-IDF=0.94 on DD contradicts this).\n18\tYES: \"We show that vanilla semantic temporal modeling fails catastrophically on unseen attack types, and that persuasion-aware fine-tuning provides an effective and accessible supervision signal for learning cross-attack-robust representations. The persuasion multi-task objective bridges the OOD generalization gap that generic encoders cannot.\"\n19\t\n20\t## Inherent Lexical Separability (D009)\n21\tPersuasive jailbreak conversations and general benign conversations are inherently lexically separable (TF-IDF≈1.0 on ALL data sources including real WildChat). TF-IDF remains strong even OOD (F1=0.94 on DD). The paper acknowledges this and positions DisPeD's value as: (1) bridging the vanilla temporal modeling gap (+72pp on DD), (2) zero degradation under adversarial perturbation (plan_004), (3) interpretability via strategy trajectories.\n22\t\n23\t## Comprehensive Ablation Picture (D014)\n24\tSystematic ablation reveals the method's actual mechanism:\n25\t- **Critical**: Persuasion-domain DeBERTa fine-tuning (any form) — bridges OOD gap from F1=0.28 to F1=1.0\n26\t- **Non-critical**: Label granularity (binary≈scrambled≈8-class, 1-6pp gap on DD), Lambda tuning (all F1=1.0), Temporal modeling (mean pooling≈GRU on DD)\n27\t- **Implication**: The persuasion multi-task objective is an effective and accessible supervision signal. Architecture and annotation precision are secondary. 8-class taxonomy provides interpretability, not detection advantage.\n28\t\n29\t## Core Claims (3, priority-ordered)\n30\t\n31\t### Claim 1 (PRIMARY): Cross-Attack Generalization via Persuasion-Aware Fine-Tuning\n32\tFine-tuning DeBERTa on persuasion-annotated conversations produces turn embeddings that enable cross-attack generalization, while vanilla semantic embeddings fail. A model trained on Crescendo detects unseen Deceptive Delight attacks with F1=0.99-1.0 while vanilla DeBERTa collapses to F1=0.28-0.58 (+42-72pp delta). The multi-task persuasion objective acts as an effective supervision signal for learning pragmatic features shared across attack types. Scrambled-label control (D010): randomizing strategy labels reduces cross-attack F1 by only 1-6pp (Scrambled F1=0.935-0.990), confirming that the fine-tuning process on persuasion-related text, not the specific label assignments, is the primary driver. This positions the persuasion taxonomy as a practical, low-annotation-barrier training objective rather than requiring precise strategy labels.\n33\t- Evidence: plan_006 DD cross-attack — Treatment F1=0.99-1.0 vs Baseline 0.28-0.58\n34\t- Evidence: plan_006 scrambled DD — Scrambled F1=0.935-0.990 (Treatment-Scrambled gap 1-6pp, both >> Baseline +36-70pp)\n35\t- Implication: 8-class taxonomy value is primarily interpretability (Claim 2), not detection performance\n36\t- Strength: strong empirical support for fine-tuning effect; labels are helpful but not essential\n37\t\n38\t### Claim 2 (ANALYSIS): Characteristic Persuasion Trajectories as Interpretability\n39\tDifferent multi-turn attack types exhibit distinct but recognizable persuasion strategy trajectories: Crescendo shows gradual escalation (mean strategy index 0.86→5.64), FITD follows commitment-escalation patterns, Deceptive Delight uses emotional-manipulation→role-assignment sequences. Benign conversations lack these structured escalation patterns. NOTE (D014): mean pooling ≈ GRU on DD, so the model does NOT rely on temporal ordering for detection. Trajectories are a descriptive/interpretability contribution, not a detection mechanism.\n40\t- Evidence: plan_008 strategy transition matrices\n41\t- Evidence: plan_015 DD — mean pooling F1=1.0 ≈ GRU F1=1.0, proving detection doesn't depend on turn ordering\n42\t- Strength: descriptive/analytical, interpretability value only; does NOT drive detection performance\n43\t\n44\t### Claim 3 (EMPIRICAL): Robustness Under OOD and Adversarial Perturbation\n45\tVanilla semantic temporal modeling is consistently the most fragile approach: catastrophic OOD failure (F1=0.28 on DD) and significant perturbation degradation (-12-15pp). Persuasion-aware fine-tuning is the most robust: F1=1.0 on unseen attacks AND zero degradation under adversarial perturbation at K≥2. TF-IDF is intermediate: strong OOD (0.94) and mostly robust under perturbation at K≥3 (-1.3pp), but degrades at early detection K=1 (-9.6pp). The strongest differentiation is at **early detection under perturbation** (K=1): Treatment -2.6pp vs TF-IDF -9.6pp vs Baseline -14.6pp.\n46\t- Evidence: plan_006 cross-attack — Treatment=1.0, TF-IDF=0.94, Baseline=0.28 on DD\n47\t- Evidence: plan_004 perturbation (D012) — Treatment 0pp at K≥2, Baseline -12-15pp, TF-IDF -1.3pp(K≥3) to -9.6pp(K=1)\n48\t- Note: Treatment vs TF-IDF gap is small at K≥3 (1.3pp, n=76). Strongest claim is at K=1-2.\n49\t- Strength: strong empirical support across both OOD and perturbation dimensions\n50\t\n51\t## Story Spine\n52\t\n53\t**Problem**: Multi-turn jailbreak attacks (Crescendo, FITD, Deceptive Delight) distribute adversarial intent across individually benign turns. Bullwinkel et al. (2025) show per-turn representations are deliberately kept in a \"benign\" region, causing representation-level defenses to fail with a 50-point ASR gap. DeepContext (Su et al., arXiv 2602.16935, 2026) tracks generic semantic drift via RNN over BERT embeddings (F1=0.84) but confuses benign conversational evolution with adversarial manipulation.\n54\t\n55\t**Gap**: Vanilla semantic temporal modeling (the natural approach: generic encoder + RNN over turn embeddings) catastrophically fails to generalize across attack types. DeepContext achieves F1=0.84 on in-distribution data but the approach collapses to F1=0.28 on unseen attack families. Production multi-turn guards (Llama Guard, WildGuard, ShieldGemma) and LLM-as-judge approaches detect individual harmful outputs but not the adversarial manipulation process. No published work uses persuasion-grounded fine-tuning to bridge this OOD generalization gap.\n56\t\n57\t**Our angle**: DisPeD uses persuasion strategy classification as a multi-task fine-tuning objective, teaching the encoder to attend to attack-relevant pragmatic features shared across attack types. Aggregation over fine-tuned turn embeddings (mean pooling or GRU, both equivalent per plan_015) enables conversation-level detection. The persuasion objective provides an accessible supervision signal: even with imperfect (scrambled) strategy labels or binary labels, fine-tuning on persuasion-related text produces representations that generalize to unseen attacks (F1=1.0 on DD), while vanilla embeddings collapse (F1=0.28). The 8-class taxonomy additionally enables native interpretability through strategy trajectory analysis.\n58\t\n59\t**Headline result**: \"Vanilla semantic temporal modeling collapses on unseen attack types (F1=0.28 on Deceptive Delight). DisPeD, fine-tuned on persuasion strategy classification, achieves F1=1.0 on the same unseen attacks (+72pp). This generalization survives label scrambling (F1 drops only 1-6pp), confirming that the persuasion fine-tuning objective, not precise annotations, bridges the cross-attack OOD gap.\"\n60\t\n61\t## Architecture\n62\t\n63\t1. **Stage 1**: DeBERTa-v3-base fine-tuned on multi-task objective: 8-class persuasion strategy classification + binary jailbreak-intent classification\n64\t2. **Stage 2**: Aggregation (GRU or mean pooling, equivalent per plan_015) over DeBERTa [CLS] embeddings for conversation-level binary classification\n65\t3. **Baseline**: Same pipeline but with vanilla (not fine-tuned) DeBERTa embeddings\n66\tNote: plan_015 shows GRU ≈ mean pooling on both IID and OOD. Paper reports GRU as default architecture but ablation shows temporal modeling is not the source of improvement.\n67\t\n68\t## Venue Fit Risks\n69\t- EMNLP 2026 LLM Security track: strong fit (dedicated track, high visibility)\n70\t- Discourse/Pragmatics reviewers may want more linguistic rigor: ground taxonomy in PAP (ACL 2024), Hagendorff (Nature Comm 2026), Cialdini principles, Speech Act Theory (Austin 1962, Searle 1969)\n71\t- Safety reviewers expect multiple attack types: need >= 2 attack families\n72\t\n73\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n74\t\n75\t### RC1: Lexical Shortcut (CRITICAL, must address pre-submission)\n76\tTF-IDF=1.0 IID and 0.94 OOD on DD. Lexical features are inherently strong for this task (D009). DisPeD cannot claim superiority over TF-IDF on raw detection performance alone.\n77\t- Mitigation strategy (post-D010): (a) acknowledge inherent lexical separability honestly; (b) DisPeD's primary value vs TF-IDF is interpretability (strategy trajectories, Claim 2) and bridging the vanilla temporal modeling gap (Claim 3); (c) plan_004 adversarial perturbation (pending): if TF-IDF degrades under paraphrasing but treatment doesn't, this demonstrates complementary robustness; (d) the core comparison that motivates DisPeD is vs vanilla DeBERTa+GRU (DeepContext approach, F1=0.28 on DD), not vs TF-IDF; (e) persuasion fine-tuning as supervision signal is the contribution, not beating a bag-of-words\n78\t\n79\t### RC2: Small Dataset\n80\t480 samples is small for a detection paper.\n81\t- Mitigation: (a) statistical significance via bootstrap CI or paired tests; (b) cross-validation; (c) focus narrative on proof-of-concept for the principle, not SOTA numbers\n82\t\n83\t### RC3: Single Attack Type (Crescendo Only)\n84\tNeed generalization evidence.\n85\t- Mitigation: generate FITD-style and Deceptive Delight-style conversations using existing pipeline\n86\t\n87\t### RC4: Strategy Label Quality (D010)\n88\tScrambled-label control (D010) shows correct strategy labels contribute only 1-6pp to cross-attack F1. This is not a confound but a finding: the persuasion multi-task objective works as a supervision signal even with imperfect labels. The 8-class taxonomy's primary value is interpretability (Claim 2), not detection.\n89\t- Mitigation: report scrambled control results transparently; position taxonomy as practical training objective with interpretability bonus\n90\t\n91\t### RC5: No Production/LLM-as-Judge Baseline (Dream-surfaced)\n92\tWithout comparing against zero-shot LLM-based detection (e.g., GPT-4/Claude prompted for conversation-level jailbreak detection), practical positioning is unknown.\n93\t- Result: GPT-5.5 (frontier LLM) achieves F1=0.19-0.45 on Crescendo (precision=1.0, recall=0.11-0.29). Qwen3-8B F1=0.0-0.15. Both far below Treatment F1=1.0. GPT-5.5 is deceived by Crescendo's \"surface educational framing,\" confirming that content-level analysis cannot detect persuasion dynamics. Unparseable rate 30-47% (increases with conversation length); paper must report this and provide unparseable-excluded upper bound. Even assuming best-case unparseable correction, LLM judge F1 ≈ 0.7-0.8, still far below Treatment=1.0. ✅ RESOLVED\n94\t\n95\t### RC6: Interpretability Claims Without Human Eval\n96\tStrategy sequence outputs claimed interpretable but not evaluated.\n97\t- Mitigation: strategy transition matrix visualization + predicted-label trajectory analysis\n98\t\n99\t### RC7: Related Work Inadequate (Dream-surfaced, THREE-TIER CONSISTENT)\n100\t~12 citations is far below venue norms (30-50). Missing coverage of: production safety systems (Llama Guard, WildGuard, ShieldGemma), LLM-as-judge, discourse analysis (RST, PDTB, dialogue acts), computational persuasion (Habernal & Gurevych 2016, Tan et al. 2016), red-teaming (HarmBench, Perez et al. 2022), annotation artifacts (Gururangan et al. 2018), adaptive attacks.\n101\t- Mitigation: expand to 30+ citations covering all missing categories (Paper Agent task)\n102\t\n103\t### RC8: Incremental Novelty Over Prior Work (Dream-surfaced, TWO-TIER)\n104\tPragmatic Evasion Asymmetry is a natural corollary of Zeng+Hagendorff+HowDoLLMs. Paper should position as \"operationalizing known observations\" not \"identifying a new phenomenon.\"\n105\t- Mitigation: reframe contribution as operationalization + empirical validation (done in novelty positioning above)\n106\t\n107\t### RC9: Multi-seed and Statistical Rigor (Dream-surfaced, THREE-TIER)\n108\tn=72 test set with single seed is inadequate.\n109\t- Result: 3-seed completed. Treatment F1=1.00±0.00 (zero variance), Baseline K=1 0.968±0.033. ✅ RESOLVED\n110\t\n111\t### RC10: \"None\"-Collapse Ablation (Dream-surfaced, TWO-TIER)\n112\tModel may reduce to persuasion-presence detector rather than trajectory detector.\n113\t- Result: Confirmed (D013/D014). Binary≈9-class on DD (1-2pp gap). Mean pooling≈GRU. Model IS a persuasion-presence detector, not a trajectory detector. Paper acknowledges this honestly; 8-class value is interpretability only. ✅ RESOLVED (acknowledged)\n114\t\n115\t### RC11: Lambda and Architecture Ablation (Dream-surfaced, TWO-TIER)\n116\tLambda and architecture choices not ablated.\n117\t- Result: Lambda∈{0,0.3,0.5,2.0} all F1=1.0 (insensitive). Mean pooling≈GRU on IID and DD. Method robust to all hyperparameter/architecture choices. ✅ RESOLVED\n118\t\n119\t## Scope Boundary\n120\t- IN: persuasion-mediated multi-turn attacks (Crescendo, FITD, Deceptive Delight, social engineering jailbreaks)\n121\t- OUT (acknowledged limitation): pure information accumulation attacks, token smuggling, single-frame roleplay\n122\t\n123\t## Theoretical Grounding\n124\t### Core References\n125\t- Bullwinkel et al. (arXiv 2507.02956): per-turn representations kept \"benign\" by multi-turn attacks\n126\t- Speech Act Theory (Austin 1962, Searle 1969): existing detectors at locutionary act level, DisPeD at illocutionary act level\n127\t- PAP (Zeng et al., ACL 2024): 40 persuasion techniques for jailbreaking\n128\t- Hagendorff (Nature Communications 2026): identifiable persuasion strategies in 97% ASR attacks\n129\t- \"How Do LLMs Persuade?\" (arXiv 2508.05625): persuasion dynamics extractable from turn-level representations\n130\t\n131\t### Must-Cite Categories (Dream-identified gaps)\n132\t- **Production safety**: Llama Guard 2/3, ShieldGemma, WildGuard, Constitutional Classifiers\n133\t- **Multi-turn defenses**: BIID, G-Guard, SafeDream\n134\t- **Discourse analysis**: RST (Mann & Thompson 1988), PDTB, dialogue act classification (Stolcke et al. 2000)\n135\t- **Computational persuasion**: Habernal & Gurevych 2016, Tan et al. 2016\n136\t- **Red-teaming**: Perez et al. 2022, Ganguli et al. 2022, HarmBench (Mazeika et al. 2024)\n137\t- **Adaptive attacks**: Nasr & Carlini et al. 2025\n138\t- **Annotation artifacts**: Gururangan et al. 2018, Niven & Kao 2019, Poliak et al. 2018\n139\t- **Jailbreak taxonomies**: Wei et al. 2024, Chao et al. 2023 (PAIR)\n140\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b3d1c62f-b1d0-462a-a314-c5cc4ac5f42a","uuid":"2927dd90-6fe1-4a31-9611-07d4c46fea3b","timestamp":"2026-05-18T03:50:55.409Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_2/projects/persuasion_turn_jailbreak_detect/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260518T032748/worst/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — persuasion_turn_jailbreak_detect\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (ARR submission)\n- Deadline: 2026-05-25 (ARR May cycle)\n- Track: LLM Security / Discourse and Pragmatics\n- Commitment deadline: 2026-08-02\n\n## Title (Working)\nDisPeD: Persuasion-Aware Fine-Tuning for Robust Cross-Attack Multi-Turn Jailbreak Detection\n\n## Core Insight\nPrior work established that persuasion is systematic in jailbreaks (Zeng et al. 2024), universal in successful attacks (Hagendorff 2026), and extractable from representations (arXiv 2508.05625). DisPeD operationalizes these observations: fine-tuning DeBERTa on an 8-class persuasion taxonomy as a multi-task objective produces turn embeddings that capture attack-relevant pragmatic features. The key finding is that vanilla semantic temporal modeling (the DeepContext-like approach: generic encoder + GRU) catastrophically fails on unseen attack types (F1=0.28 on DD), while persuasion-aware fine-tuning enables robust cross-attack generalization (F1=1.0). Even scrambled strategy labels achieve F1=0.98, confirming that the fine-tuning process on persuasion-related text, not specific label assignments, is the critical ingredient. The 8-class taxonomy additionally provides native interpretability via strategy trajectory analysis.\n\n## Novelty Positioning (Dream-informed)\nNOT: \"We discover that persuasion patterns exist in jailbreaks\" (already known).\nNOT: \"An evasion hierarchy exists: lexical < semantic < pragmatic\" (TF-IDF=0.94 on DD contradicts this).\nYES: \"We show that vanilla semantic temporal modeling fails catastrophically on unseen attack types, and that persuasion-aware fine-tuning provides an effective and accessible supervision signal for learning cross-attack-robust representations. The persuasion multi-task objective bridges the OOD generalization gap that generic encoders cannot.\"\n\n## Inherent Lexical Separability (D009)\nPersuasive jailbreak conversations and general benign conversations are inherently lexically separable (TF-IDF≈1.0 on ALL data sources including real WildChat). TF-IDF remains strong even OOD (F1=0.94 on DD). The paper acknowledges this and positions DisPeD's value as: (1) bridging the vanilla temporal modeling gap (+72pp on DD), (2) zero degradation under adversarial perturbation (plan_004), (3) interpretability via strategy trajectories.\n\n## Comprehensive Ablation Picture (D014)\nSystematic ablation reveals the method's actual mechanism:\n- **Critical**: Persuasion-domain DeBERTa fine-tuning (any form) — bridges OOD gap from F1=0.28 to F1=1.0\n- **Non-critical**: Label granularity (binary≈scrambled≈8-class, 1-6pp gap on DD), Lambda tuning (all F1=1.0), Temporal modeling (mean pooling≈GRU on DD)\n- **Implication**: The persuasion multi-task objective is an effective and accessible supervision signal. Architecture and annotation precision are secondary. 8-class taxonomy provides interpretability, not detection advantage.\n\n## Core Claims (3, priority-ordered)\n\n### Claim 1 (PRIMARY): Cross-Attack Generalization via Persuasion-Aware Fine-Tuning\nFine-tuning DeBERTa on persuasion-annotated conversations produces turn embeddings that enable cross-attack generalization, while vanilla semantic embeddings fail. A model trained on Crescendo detects unseen Deceptive Delight attacks with F1=0.99-1.0 while vanilla DeBERTa collapses to F1=0.28-0.58 (+42-72pp delta). The multi-task persuasion objective acts as an effective supervision signal for learning pragmatic features shared across attack types. Scrambled-label control (D010): randomizing strategy labels reduces cross-attack F1 by only 1-6pp (Scrambled F1=0.935-0.990), confirming that the fine-tuning process on persuasion-related text, not the specific label assignments, is the primary driver. This positions the persuasion taxonomy as a practical, low-annotation-barrier training objective rather than requiring precise strategy labels.\n- Evidence: plan_006 DD cross-attack — Treatment F1=0.99-1.0 vs Baseline 0.28-0.58\n- Evidence: plan_006 scrambled DD — Scrambled F1=0.935-0.990 (Treatment-Scrambled gap 1-6pp, both >> Baseline +36-70pp)\n- Implication: 8-class taxonomy value is primarily interpretability (Claim 2), not detection performance\n- Strength: strong empirical support for fine-tuning effect; labels are helpful but not essential\n\n### Claim 2 (ANALYSIS): Characteristic Persuasion Trajectories as Interpretability\nDifferent multi-turn attack types exhibit distinct but recognizable persuasion strategy trajectories: Crescendo shows gradual escalation (mean strategy index 0.86→5.64), FITD follows commitment-escalation patterns, Deceptive Delight uses emotional-manipulation→role-assignment sequences. Benign conversations lack these structured escalation patterns. NOTE (D014): mean pooling ≈ GRU on DD, so the model does NOT rely on temporal ordering for detection. Trajectories are a descriptive/interpretability contribution, not a detection mechanism.\n- Evidence: plan_008 strategy transition matrices\n- Evidence: plan_015 DD — mean pooling F1=1.0 ≈ GRU F1=1.0, proving detection doesn't depend on turn ordering\n- Strength: descriptive/analytical, interpretability value only; does NOT drive detection performance\n\n### Claim 3 (EMPIRICAL): Robustness Under OOD and Adversarial Perturbation\nVanilla semantic temporal modeling is consistently the most fragile approach: catastrophic OOD failure (F1=0.28 on DD) and significant perturbation degradation (-12-15pp). Persuasion-aware fine-tuning is the most robust: F1=1.0 on unseen attacks AND zero degradation under adversarial perturbation at K≥2. TF-IDF is intermediate: strong OOD (0.94) and mostly robust under perturbation at K≥3 (-1.3pp), but degrades at early detection K=1 (-9.6pp). The strongest differentiation is at **early detection under perturbation** (K=1): Treatment -2.6pp vs TF-IDF -9.6pp vs Baseline -14.6pp.\n- Evidence: plan_006 cross-attack — Treatment=1.0, TF-IDF=0.94, Baseline=0.28 on DD\n- Evidence: plan_004 perturbation (D012) — Treatment 0pp at K≥2, Baseline -12-15pp, TF-IDF -1.3pp(K≥3) to -9.6pp(K=1)\n- Note: Treatment vs TF-IDF gap is small at K≥3 (1.3pp, n=76). Strongest claim is at K=1-2.\n- Strength: strong empirical support across both OOD and perturbation dimensions\n\n## Story Spine\n\n**Problem**: Multi-turn jailbreak attacks (Crescendo, FITD, Deceptive Delight) distribute adversarial intent across individually benign turns. Bullwinkel et al. (2025) show per-turn representations are deliberately kept in a \"benign\" region, causing representation-level defenses to fail with a 50-point ASR gap. DeepContext (Su et al., arXiv 2602.16935, 2026) tracks generic semantic drift via RNN over BERT embeddings (F1=0.84) but confuses benign conversational evolution with adversarial manipulation.\n\n**Gap**: Vanilla semantic temporal modeling (the natural approach: generic encoder + RNN over turn embeddings) catastrophically fails to generalize across attack types. DeepContext achieves F1=0.84 on in-distribution data but the approach collapses to F1=0.28 on unseen attack families. Production multi-turn guards (Llama Guard, WildGuard, ShieldGemma) and LLM-as-judge approaches detect individual harmful outputs but not the adversarial manipulation process. No published work uses persuasion-grounded fine-tuning to bridge this OOD generalization gap.\n\n**Our angle**: DisPeD uses persuasion strategy classification as a multi-task fine-tuning objective, teaching the encoder to attend to attack-relevant pragmatic features shared across attack types. Aggregation over fine-tuned turn embeddings (mean pooling or GRU, both equivalent per plan_015) enables conversation-level detection. The persuasion objective provides an accessible supervision signal: even with imperfect (scrambled) strategy labels or binary labels, fine-tuning on persuasion-related text produces representations that generalize to unseen attacks (F1=1.0 on DD), while vanilla embeddings collapse (F1=0.28). The 8-class taxonomy additionally enables native interpretability through strategy trajectory analysis.\n\n**Headline result**: \"Vanilla semantic temporal modeling collapses on unseen attack types (F1=0.28 on Deceptive Delight). DisPeD, fine-tuned on persuasion strategy classification, achieves F1=1.0 on the same unseen attacks (+72pp). This generalization survives label scrambling (F1 drops only 1-6pp), confirming that the persuasion fine-tuning objective, not precise annotations, bridges the cross-attack OOD gap.\"\n\n## Architecture\n\n1. **Stage 1**: DeBERTa-v3-base fine-tuned on multi-task objective: 8-class persuasion strategy classification + binary jailbreak-intent classification\n2. **Stage 2**: Aggregation (GRU or mean pooling, equivalent per plan_015) over DeBERTa [CLS] embeddings for conversation-level binary classification\n3. **Baseline**: Same pipeline but with vanilla (not fine-tuned) DeBERTa embeddings\nNote: plan_015 shows GRU ≈ mean pooling on both IID and OOD. Paper reports GRU as default architecture but ablation shows temporal modeling is not the source of improvement.\n\n## Venue Fit Risks\n- EMNLP 2026 LLM Security track: strong fit (dedicated track, high visibility)\n- Discourse/Pragmatics reviewers may want more linguistic rigor: ground taxonomy in PAP (ACL 2024), Hagendorff (Nature Comm 2026), Cialdini principles, Speech Act Theory (Austin 1962, Searle 1969)\n- Safety reviewers expect multiple attack types: need >= 2 attack families\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n\n### RC1: Lexical Shortcut (CRITICAL, must address pre-submission)\nTF-IDF=1.0 IID and 0.94 OOD on DD. Lexical features are inherently strong for this task (D009). DisPeD cannot claim superiority over TF-IDF on raw detection performance alone.\n- Mitigation strategy (post-D010): (a) acknowledge inherent lexical separability honestly; (b) DisPeD's primary value vs TF-IDF is interpretability (strategy trajectories, Claim 2) and bridging the vanilla temporal modeling gap (Claim 3); (c) plan_004 adversarial perturbation (pending): if TF-IDF degrades under paraphrasing but treatment doesn't, this demonstrates complementary robustness; (d) the core comparison that motivates DisPeD is vs vanilla DeBERTa+GRU (DeepContext approach, F1=0.28 on DD), not vs TF-IDF; (e) persuasion fine-tuning as supervision signal is the contribution, not beating a bag-of-words\n\n### RC2: Small Dataset\n480 samples is small for a detection paper.\n- Mitigation: (a) statistical significance via bootstrap CI or paired tests; (b) cross-validation; (c) focus narrative on proof-of-concept for the principle, not SOTA numbers\n\n### RC3: Single Attack Type (Crescendo Only)\nNeed generalization evidence.\n- Mitigation: generate FITD-style and Deceptive Delight-style conversations using existing pipeline\n\n### RC4: Strategy Label Quality (D010)\nScrambled-label control (D010) shows correct strategy labels contribute only 1-6pp to cross-attack F1. This is not a confound but a finding: the persuasion multi-task objective works as a supervision signal even with imperfect labels. The 8-class taxonomy's primary value is interpretability (Claim 2), not detection.\n- Mitigation: report scrambled control results transparently; position taxonomy as practical training objective with interpretability bonus\n\n### RC5: No Production/LLM-as-Judge Baseline (Dream-surfaced)\nWithout comparing against zero-shot LLM-based detection (e.g., GPT-4/Claude prompted for conversation-level jailbreak detection), practical positioning is unknown.\n- Result: GPT-5.5 (frontier LLM) achieves F1=0.19-0.45 on Crescendo (precision=1.0, recall=0.11-0.29). Qwen3-8B F1=0.0-0.15. Both far below Treatment F1=1.0. GPT-5.5 is deceived by Crescendo's \"surface educational framing,\" confirming that content-level analysis cannot detect persuasion dynamics. Unparseable rate 30-47% (increases with conversation length); paper must report this and provide unparseable-excluded upper bound. Even assuming best-case unparseable correction, LLM judge F1 ≈ 0.7-0.8, still far below Treatment=1.0. ✅ RESOLVED\n\n### RC6: Interpretability Claims Without Human Eval\nStrategy sequence outputs claimed interpretable but not evaluated.\n- Mitigation: strategy transition matrix visualization + predicted-label trajectory analysis\n\n### RC7: Related Work Inadequate (Dream-surfaced, THREE-TIER CONSISTENT)\n~12 citations is far below venue norms (30-50). Missing coverage of: production safety systems (Llama Guard, WildGuard, ShieldGemma), LLM-as-judge, discourse analysis (RST, PDTB, dialogue acts), computational persuasion (Habernal & Gurevych 2016, Tan et al. 2016), red-teaming (HarmBench, Perez et al. 2022), annotation artifacts (Gururangan et al. 2018), adaptive attacks.\n- Mitigation: expand to 30+ citations covering all missing categories (Paper Agent task)\n\n### RC8: Incremental Novelty Over Prior Work (Dream-surfaced, TWO-TIER)\nPragmatic Evasion Asymmetry is a natural corollary of Zeng+Hagendorff+HowDoLLMs. Paper should position as \"operationalizing known observations\" not \"identifying a new phenomenon.\"\n- Mitigation: reframe contribution as operationalization + empirical validation (done in novelty positioning above)\n\n### RC9: Multi-seed and Statistical Rigor (Dream-surfaced, THREE-TIER)\nn=72 test set with single seed is inadequate.\n- Result: 3-seed completed. Treatment F1=1.00±0.00 (zero variance), Baseline K=1 0.968±0.033. ✅ RESOLVED\n\n### RC10: \"None\"-Collapse Ablation (Dream-surfaced, TWO-TIER)\nModel may reduce to persuasion-presence detector rather than trajectory detector.\n- Result: Confirmed (D013/D014). Binary≈9-class on DD (1-2pp gap). Mean pooling≈GRU. Model IS a persuasion-presence detector, not a trajectory detector. Paper acknowledges this honestly; 8-class value is interpretability only. ✅ RESOLVED (acknowledged)\n\n### RC11: Lambda and Architecture Ablation (Dream-surfaced, TWO-TIER)\nLambda and architecture choices not ablated.\n- Result: Lambda∈{0,0.3,0.5,2.0} all F1=1.0 (insensitive). Mean pooling≈GRU on IID and DD. Method robust to all hyperparameter/architecture choices. ✅ RESOLVED\n\n## Scope Boundary\n- IN: persuasion-mediated multi-turn attacks (Crescendo, FITD, Deceptive Delight, social engineering jailbreaks)\n- OUT (acknowledged limitation): pure information accumulation attacks, token smuggling, single-frame roleplay\n\n## Theoretical Grounding\n### Core References\n- Bullwinkel et al. (arXiv 2507.02956): per-turn representations kept \"benign\" by multi-turn attacks\n- Speech Act Theory (Austin 1962, Searle 1969): existing detectors at locutionary act level, DisPeD at illocutionary act level\n- PAP (Zeng et al., ACL 2024): 40 persuasion techniques for jailbreaking\n- Hagendorff (Nature Communications 2026): identifiable persuasion strategies in 97% ASR attacks\n- \"How Do LLMs Persuade?\" (arXiv 2508.05625): persuasion dynamics extractable from turn-level representations\n\n### Must-Cite Categories (Dream-identified gaps)\n- **Production safety**: Llama Guard 2/3, ShieldGemma, WildGuard, Constitutional Classifiers\n- **Multi-turn defenses**: BIID, G-Guard, SafeDream\n- **Discourse analysis**: RST (Mann & Thompson 1988), PDTB, dialogue act classification (Stolcke et al. 2000)\n- **Computational persuasion**: Habernal & Gurevych 2016, Tan et al. 2016\n- **Red-teaming**: Perez et al. 2022, Ganguli et al. 2022, HarmBench (Mazeika et al. 2024)\n- **Adaptive attacks**: Nasr & Carlini et al. 2025\n- **Annotation artifacts**: Gururangan et al. 2018, Niven & Kao 2019, Poliak et al. 2018\n- **Jailbreak taxonomies**: Wei et al. 2024, Chao et al. 2023 (PAIR)\n","numLines":140,"startLine":1,"totalLines":140}}}
